OPENSSL.EXE: the openssl command (OpenSSL 3.6.5) for DOS on vmdos's own TCP.
Its libraries are also LYNX.EXE's HTTPS (third_party/lynx).

    OPENSSL VERSION -a
    OPENSSL DGST -sha256 FILE.ZIP
    OPENSSL RAND -hex 16
    OPENSSL GENPKEY -algorithm ED25519 -out KEY.PEM
    OPENSSL REQ -new -x509 -key KEY.PEM -subj /CN=me -days 365 -out CERT.PEM
    OPENSSL X509 -in CERT.PEM -noout -text
    OPENSSL VERIFY -CAfile CA.PEM CERT.PEM
    OPENSSL ENC -aes-256-cbc -pbkdf2 -in FILE -out FILE.ENC
    OPENSSL S_CLIENT -connect example.com:443     (after STARTNET)

The command's name may be in capitals (OPENSSL VERSION); options are as
OpenSSL has them (-in, not -IN). OPENSSL HELP lists the commands.

Built from github.com/openssl/openssl tag openssl-3.6.5 (Apache-2.0), its
DJGPP target: static, no threads, DSOs or engines; DJGPP gcc 12 with
OpenSSL's x86 assembly; UPX --best.

Files. C:\VMDOS\NET is OpenSSL's directory (OPENSSLDIR, /dev/c/VMDOS/NET to
DJGPP): OPENSSL.CNF there is OpenSSL's sample configuration (what REQ and
CA use; OPENSSL_CONF=file for another). Certificates are checked against
%SSL_CERT_FILE%, C:\VMDOS\NET\CACERT.PEM unless set (third_party/cacert, as
WGET); -CAfile overrides.

The files here:
  openssl-dos.patch  the changes to OpenSSL: its random numbers seeded by
                     getentropy() (DOS has no /dev/urandom, DJGPP isn't
                     ELF); sockets made non-blocking by ioctlsocket()
                     (DJGPP's fcntl() doesn't know them); the command's
                     name in any case
  ossl-dos.c         what OpenSSL's DJGPP port wants from Watt-32 and from
                     DOS, on ssh's sockets (third_party/openssh/dos-sock.c):
                     read_s, write_s, close_s, send, recv, select_s and
                     select (the console is ready when a key is waiting),
                     ioctlsocket, sock_init, gethostbyname; getentropy()
                     (RDSEED/RDRAND, TSC-against-8254 jitter, the clocks);
                     the clock. Shared with LYNX.EXE.
  dos-time.h         forced into every OpenSSL (and Lynx) file: time(),
                     gettimeofday(), gmtime(), localtime() are ossl-dos.c's,
                     the DOS clock taken as UTC (as ssh). DJGPP's own got
                     the date wrong here: certificates made "in 2027",
                     good ones "expired".
  OPENSSL.CNF        OpenSSL's apps/openssl.cnf (CR LF)

Networking is ssh's (third_party/openssh/README.vmdos): vmdos runs the TCP
connections itself, IPv4 and IPv6, names from VMTCP.CFG's DNS servers.
Not there: listening sockets (S_SERVER), UDP (DTLS), engines and providers
loaded from files.

Build: tools/build-openssl.sh writes bin/OPENSSL.EXE and OPENSSL.CNF (the
normal build doesn't need it: the binary is committed); the Makefile puts
them in C:\VMDOS\NET when they're there. It needs DJGPP (a cross gcc
i586-pc-msdosdjgpp-gcc on PATH, or DJGPP=dir), git, make, perl, a host gcc
with -m32, python3 and upx; it fetches OpenSSL and Watt-32 (its socket
headers only) at the pinned versions. OPENSSL_PREFIX=dir NO_EXE=1 builds
just the libraries and headers (tools/build-lynx.sh does).
